base=Path('/mnt/data');out=Path(__file__).parent
odd=(base/'short-relation-path/research-note.md').read_text();three=(base/'three-block-path/research-note.md').read_text();even=(base/'even-fourier-path/research-note.md').read_text()
head='''# Uniform average mixing on paths: a complete arithmetic classification (revised candidate proof)

Working manuscript, 27 November 2026. AI-assisted, no personal author assigned. This revised proof has undergone internal cross-checks but not independent peer review or full Lean verification. No public repository has been changed.

## Abstract
We prove a candidate classification of uniform average mixing for continuous-time adjacency walks on paths. For a path on N=q−1 vertices, the only forbidden moduli are multiples of 15 or 21, and multiples of 6 at least 12. The negative direction and the forced-coherence reduction are due to Kang (2026). The new positive direction uses strictly positive Fourier densities on cyclotomic phase groups, together with a classification of low-weight signed root-of-unity relations. A square-completion inequality and exact small-dimensional Fourier calculations eliminate the earlier reliance on linear-programming probability certificates in all fourteen exceptional moduli. The remaining finite computations are integer lattice enumeration and rational Fourier coefficients. We also establish exact finite readout laws on arbitrary prescribed nonnegative grids with positive algebraic step.

## Main theorem
For each integer q>=3, with N=q−1, let A denote the adjacency matrix of P_N. There exists a probability measure mu with E_mu |exp(itA)|^{circ 2}=J_N/N if and only if 15 does not divide q, 21 does not divide q, and either 6 does not divide q or q=6. For every admitted q, every algebraic delta>0 and every t0>=0, such a measure can be chosen with finite support in t0+delta Z_{>=0}. The support has at most m²+1 points for odd q, m=(q−1)/2, and m(m+1)+1 for even q, m=q/2−1. These bounds are not asserted minimal. The negative direction follows from Kang, arXiv:2609.14463v1; the positive direction outside his positive classes is the contribution here. The following parts supply the full candidate positive proof, without using any earlier general-family conjectures or numerical LP witnesses.

## Common reduction and short-relation localization
For theta_r=2 cos(r pi/q), the sine spectral projectors give E_mu M=D0+2 sum_{r<s} y_rs E_r∘E_s with D0=(2J+I+S)/(2q), S the reflection. The target y_rs=−1/N for equal mirror parity, and 0 otherwise, gives J/N because the sum of the entrywise squared parity projector sums is (I+S)/2. Kang proves necessity and the parity-collision obstructions. Here only sufficiency needs to be constructed.

The relevant compact phase groups are the closures of the real energy flow; their annihilator lattices are exactly the integer spectral relations. A real character cosine has Haar mean zero and squared norm 1/2 if its frequency is nonzero; distinct frequency classes modulo sign are orthogonal. Thus for a real nonnegative Fourier polynomial F, its actual mean Z_H and actual moments M_v, a scalar T>0, and target y_v, the polynomial

    f=[F+(T−Z_H)+2 sum_v(T y_v−M_v) Re chi_v]/T

has mass one and exactly the target moments if the target cosines are nontrivial and orthogonal. A sufficient positivity condition is T−Z_H>2 sum_v |T y_v−M_v|. Stronger pointwise lower bounds on the correction may be used instead, as in q=33 below. Full support is essential for the passage to an actual finite time law.

We give an explicit justification of the localization step used in the three proof parts. A finite signed rational root array of zero evaluation is partitioned by successively removing nonempty vanishing subsets minimal under inclusion with the original coefficients. This process preserves the original weights; no coefficient is split and the supports are disjoint. For each minimal subset the Conway–Jones theorem (signed rational version, also Lenstra survey Theorem 2.6) says its reduced order d is squarefree and its support cardinality is >=2+sum_{p|d}(p−2). All exponents are in a single coset of the subgroup of order d. In the odd case use the cyclic group Z/q directly. In the even case put zeta=exp(pi i/q). Exponents ±r, 0<r<q/2, are distinct modulo q. Reducing modulo q identifies antipodal roots, absorbing the sign into the integer coefficient. A squarefree reduced order d' in the order-2q group projects to order d=d'/gcd(2,d'), which is odd and squarefree. The support size of each block is unchanged and the factor 2 contributes zero to the Conway–Jones bound. For a coset of order d, choose a representative a and lift its entries as zeta^a times d-th roots; signs are absorbed exactly. If every block order divides d, every block stays entirely in one d-coset; the restriction of the root array to that coset vanishes after this lift. This applies also if the original choice of minimal partition was not invariant under inversion. Reflection on cosets then follows from inversion symmetry of the *whole* array. A stable odd-order coset in Z/q contains exactly one solution of 2x=0, since multiplication by two is bijective on its odd-order subgroup. The fixed point is 0 in the odd case, and either 0 or q/2 in the even case. Both are absent from the positive-mode spectral array. In an even central lift, the common factors at the two fixed points are respectively 1 and i; the corresponding coefficient arrays obey A(−x)=A(x) and A(−x)=−A(x). Noncentral cosets are paired with their inverse and have equal absolute weight. Complete prime cycles are forced by irreducibility of Phi_p. Pure cycles of distinct prime orders intersect in at most one point, while two cosets of the same order coincide or are disjoint. These facts justify the pure-cycle pairing and central reduction used below. Support cardinality is always bounded above by absolute coefficient weight, and root absolute weight is twice spectral L1 weight. In the mixed case the relevant orders are read off from the Conway–Jones inequality and the total budget 20; the detailed partitions and multiplicities are established in the corresponding parts. This lemma does not assume symmetry of the minimal partition.
'''
def portion(t,a,b):return t[t.index(a):t.index(b)]
def demote(t):return '\n'.join('#'+line if line.startswith('##') else line for line in t.splitlines())+'\n'
A=portion(odd,'## 2. Spectral','## 5. Real')
B=portion(three,'## 2. Phase','## 7. Passage')
B=B.replace('one 1, four −1 | none | 10 | +2 | 5','one 1, four −1 (two coefficients of magnitude 2) | none | 10 | +2 | 5')
B=B.replace('For q=33 the same density\'s sufficient L1 margin is negative (−643381/4096); this is NOT a nonexistence result. We use the separate certificate in Section 6 instead.', 'For q=33 the separate L1 bound is negative (−643381/4096), but Section 6 supplies a sharper square-completion bound proving the same density strictly positive.')
start=B.index('For q=33 the archived exact rational certificate')
B=B[:start]+'''For q=33 no probability certificate is needed. There are five triples, with sums s_j=z_{j,1}+z_{j,2}+z_{j,3}, and a singleton z0. Write A_c=V−U=42107/4096>0 and B_c=V−W=40931/4096. The two conjugate correction sums in the numerator of f, per triple, equal

    A_c(|s_j|²−3)+2 B_c Re(s_j conjugate(z0))
      = A_c |s_j+(B_c/A_c) z0|²−3A_c−B_c²/A_c.

Hence the combined five-triple correction is bounded below by −5(3A_c+B_c²/A_c), rather than the old separate L1 bound −30(A_c+B_c). Exact independent cyclotomic enumeration (134245 free vectors of length at most 10, 352 nonzero short relations) and multinomial coefficient extraction give Z=16555999/4096, U=−1342297/8192, V=−1258083/8192, W=−1339945/8192, G=3573329/4096 and E_extra=862785/2048. Therefore

    G−5(3A_c+B_c²/A_c)−E_extra = 42831862673/172470272 > 0.

All 256 target moments and the normalization were checked exactly. This proves positivity of the original three-block density at q=33, independent of the archived LP certificate. See verify_q33_density.py. The failed earlier separate L1 bound and exploratory polynomial optimization are not counterexamples.

'''
C=portion(even,'## 2. Spectral','## 6. The finite')
start=C.index('If a central mixed component is accompanied')
C=C[:start]+'''The inventory above also has a short analytic proof. In the symmetric case with unequal a1,a2, the length bound L>=6|a1−a2| implies they differ by one. If both are nonzero with the same sign, each column costs at least 2+min(|a1|,|a2|), and the total is at least 12. Up to row swap and global sign the pair is (0,1); b entries are 0 or −1, since any other costs at least 7 and the other two columns cost at least 2 each. The nonzero-axis condition leaves k=1,2,3 entries of −1, giving 4 binomial(3,k) arrays of respective lengths 8,9,10, all unit coefficients. For equal a1=a2=A!=0, each column costs at least |A|, so |A|<=2. At |A|=2 all b_j=−A. At A=1 up to sign, all b_j=−1, except possibly one 0 or one −2; the cost increments are 3 and 5 respectively from baseline 1. This gives 2 length-five, 2 five-double, 6 length-eight and 6 mixed-double arrays. Thus the symmetric table is exactly 44. In the antisymmetric case each column costs |b|+2 max(|a1|,|b|)+2 max(|a2|,|b|). Therefore L>=7(|a1|+|a2|); with both axes nonzero the row pair has precisely one entry ±1. A nonzero column adds at least 3, so exactly one column is ±1 and others zero. The 2*2*3*2=24 arrays have eight unit coefficients and one double. This proves the full inventory without enumeration.\n\n'''+C[start:]
C=C.replace('Exhaustion of the 243 tuples proves this impossible (verify_classification.py). This is a finite check with analytically proved parameter bounds.', '''The half-weight is |a1−1|+|a2−1|+sum_j(|b_j|+2|a1+b_j|+2|a2+b_j|). If either a is 2, all b must be −1 and the weight is at least 10. If both a are zero it is 2+5k; if one is zero and one is one it is at least 7; if both are one it is 3+3k (k>=0 integer). None equals 5. Thus no enumeration is needed, although verify_classification.py independently confirms all 243 tuples.''')
finite='''### 6. The finite exceptional even moduli: a uniform density proof
For q>=142 both parity groups have n>=35 and the preceding global estimate applies. For q<142, all odd primes >=11 give the relation-free construction when n>=2; Kang's prime, twice-prime and power-of-two cases include the remaining small trivial groups. The only leftover moduli are 20,28,40,50,56,70,80,98,100,110,112,130,140. We now use the same F and f from Sections 3 and 5 at each of these, rather than separate rational phase laws. For each group compute its full short relation set using the classified coset templates. Independently, form the integer matrix whose columns are the coefficient vectors of zeta^r+zeta^(2q−r) reduced modulo Phi_(2q). Its kernel is exactly the relation lattice. In exact rational RREF, pivot coordinates are determined by the free coordinates; every vector of L1 length <=10 has free coordinates of L1 length <=10. Enumerate every such free integer vector and retain those whose reconstructed pivots are integral and full length <=10. The two independently generated lists agree for all 26 groups. The exhaustive free-vector count in dimension d is sum_{k=0}^{min(d,10)} 2^k binomial(d,k) binomial(10,k), including zero; this count is checked for every run. The full enumeration visited 30,094,218 free vectors, finding 1,032 nonzero short relations across these groups.\n\nFor each group let E0 and E_v be the signed deviations from independent-circle Fourier moments. Use the exact coefficients derived in Section 3, or equivalently the independent rational convolution in verify_small_density.py. Set G=−NU−Z, and compute the lower bound G−|E0|−2 sum_v |M_v−M_v^*−E_v|. This uses the combined correction rather than the loose universal error bound, and it is strictly positive in every group. The exact fractions are as follows (unnormalized density numerator lower bounds).\n\n| q | b=0 | b=1 |\n|---:|---:|---:|\n'''
for l in (out/'exact-density-margins.txt').read_text().splitlines():
 q,a,b=l.split();finite+=f'| {q} | {a} | {b} |\n'
finite+='\nThus all thirteen small even moduli use the same positive density as the general construction. This removes the need for any large probability matrix certificate. Verification consists of rational Fourier sums and exhaustive integer lattice checks, not a numerical optimizer. The coset-template generator is the fast route; RREF is an independent check of the enumeration.\n'
end='''## Passage to actual readout times and final conclusion
Every positive construction above gives a full-support phase probability on the energy orbit closure H (including the explicit q=9 law mixed with Haar). For the known positive classes, Kang's ramp with amplitude factor >1 mixed with Haar gives the same full-support property; q=3 has the required target under Haar directly. If C is the convex hull of the finite vector of cosines on H, a full-support mean lies in ri C: a supporting linear functional attaining its extremum at that mean must be constant throughout H by continuity. For delta>0 algebraic and any integer coefficient vector c, delta c·theta is algebraic, so delta c·theta belongs to 2pi Z iff c·theta=0 by transcendence of pi. Kronecker's character criterion implies that the grid orbit has the same closure as the continuous orbit; nonnegative iterates are dense in the same compact group by recurrence. Translation by t0 stays in H. The convex hull of grid cosine vectors has closure C and contains ri C, so the target is realized by a finite convex combination of actual grid vectors. Caratheodory's theorem bounds its support by m²+1 for odd q and m(m+1)+1 for even q from the number of possible positive-mode gap characters, including zero-mode gaps in the latter. Zero weights can be removed. Thus the result is existence of a finite exact time law, not a concrete short waiting-time schedule. All moduli q>=3 are covered by the three positive parts and Kang's negative theorem.\n\n## Verification and limitations\nThe symbolic moment and global positivity checks from the three original notes remain in the accompanying verification sources. The revised small cases no longer use any LP probability certificates. The C++ enumeration uses bounded integer inputs; verify_lattice.py checks the free-vector count, integrality, and every accepted relation against the exact cyclotomic matrix. q33-density.log records the exact square-completion check. These are computational mathematical checks, not a Lean kernel proof or independent peer review. The analytical short-relation localization is stated explicitly above and deserves external review. The priority search on 27 November 2026 found Kang's v1 still giving only the earlier positive and negative families, and no matching complete positive theorem. Related classical vanishing-sum classifications are prior work; novelty is claimed only provisionally for the specific positive construction and complete classification. No personal author assigned.\n\n## References\n1. M. Kang, Arithmetic of Bohr Frequencies Governs Uniform Mixing in Randomly Timed Quantum Spin Chains, arXiv:2609.14463v1 (2026), https://arxiv.org/html/2609.14463v1.\n2. P. Baptista, G. Coutinho, V. Marques, Unexpected Averages of Mixing Matrices, arXiv:2308.16378 (2023), https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.16378.\n3. J. H. Conway, A. J. Jones, Trigonometric diophantine equations (On vanishing sums of roots of unity), Acta Arith. 30 (1976), 229–240, https://doi.org/10.4064/aa-30-3-229-240.\n4. H. W. Lenstra Jr., Vanishing sums of roots of unity, Theorem 2.6, https://pub.math.leidenuniv.nl/~lenstrahw/PUBLICATIONS/1979d/art.pdf.\n5. M. Kang, Moment Methods for Uniform Average Mixing on Strongly Regular Graphs, arXiv:2610.10017 (2026), https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.10017.\n'''
